// A libc allocator reached from a `@noheap` region is refused WITH NO `@heap` MARK ON IT (KR-74).
//
// This is new, and it is the half of the row that strengthens rather than relaxes. The proof no longer asks a
// declaration what its C does: it reads the C. A call the compiler WRITES — which a call to an `extern fn` is,
// whatever the author declared — names `calloc` in the emitted text, and the name of a foreign allocator is an
// allocation fact wherever it is spelled. So the author who forgets the attribute, or who never knew about it,
// gets the same verdict as the author who remembers.
//
// The companion is noheap_heap_extern: `@heap` still exists and is still load-bearing, for a symbol the
// compiler has no such name for.
extern "<stdlib.h>";
extern fn UnsafePtr calloc(usize n, usize size);
unsafe fn int32 grab() { UnsafePtr p = calloc(n: 1, size: 4); return 0; }
@noheap fn int32 tick() { return grab(); }
fn int32 main() { return tick(); }
